{
 "id": 81,
 "number": 133,
 "title": "Clone Graph",
 "slug": "clone-graph",
 "category": "graphs",
 "difficulty": "Medium",
 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/clone-graph/",
 "pattern": "DFS + hash map (original → copy)",
 "statement": "You are given one node of a connected undirected graph. Every node has a value and a list of its neighbours. Return a **deep copy** of the whole graph: brand-new nodes with the same values and the same connections.",
 "example": {
  "input": "adjList = [[2,4],[1,3],[2,4],[1,3]]   (node 1 is linked to 2 and 4, node 2 to 1 and 3, …)",
  "output": "a new graph with the same shape: [[2,4],[1,3],[2,4],[1,3]]",
  "why": "The copy has four new nodes wired exactly like the original, and shares no node with it."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "node = Node(1)",
   "output": "[[]]"
  },
  {
   "input": "node = None",
   "output": "[]"
  },
  {
   "input": "node = Node(1)",
   "output": "[[2],[1]]"
  },
  {
   "input": "node = Node(1)",
   "output": "[[2,3],[1,3],[1,2]]"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Walk the graph with DFS and create one new node for every original node.",
  "Graphs have cycles, so the walk will come back to nodes it has already seen. A hash map **original → copy** answers two questions at once: *have I copied this node?* and *where is its copy?*",
  "Register the copy in the map **before** visiting the neighbours. Then when a cycle leads back here, the existing copy is returned instead of starting an endless loop."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def cloneGraph(self, node: \"Node\") -> \"Node\":\n        if not node:\n            return None\n\n        visited = {}  # Dictionary to store the cloned nodes\n\n        def dfs(original_node):\n            if original_node in visited:\n                return visited[original_node]\n\n            new_node = Node(original_node.val)\n            visited[original_node] = new_node\n\n            for neighbor in original_node.neighbors:\n                new_neighbor = dfs(neighbor)\n                new_node.neighbors.append(new_neighbor)\n\n            return new_node\n\n        return dfs(node)",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(V + E)",
  "timeWhy": "Every node is copied once and every edge is followed once from each side.",
  "space": "O(V)",
  "spaceWhy": "The map holds one entry per node, and the recursion can be V deep."
 },
